        "title": "03 - Age of Exploration",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The period from roughly 1400 to 1800 produced the most consequential cartographic output in history. Two intellectual catalysts converged: the rediscovery of Ptolemy's coordinate framework (which gave explorers a conceptual model for where undiscovered land *should* be) and the systematic accumulation of empirical coastal surveys (which produced the actual data). The result was not simply better maps — it was a complete reconception of the world's geography, organized, monetized, and classified as strategic intelligence by European nation-states."
    },
